WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:14.440
Across the universe, an endless war rages a
bitter struggle between invisible forces.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:23.470
Dark matter and dark energy battling for
control of the universe.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.790
We've seen this cosmic battle go on for the
past 14 billion years.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.000
Like two navies fighting it out in the ocean
of space time.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:38.140
Shadowy forces dictating our past, our
present, and our future.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:42.910
The dark universe is kind of a puppet master
behind the scenes guiding the universe that

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:43.570
we can see.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:50.070
It's taken over the universe and eventually
it may well destroy the universe.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:58.630
As we look out across the universe.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.870
We see nebulas stars.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.930
And planets all constructed from visible
matter.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.120
But what we see is just a small part of the
cosmos.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.460
The rest is invisible.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:24.050
Unknown. The dark universe.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:31.680
Dark universe is a very common phrase to
describe our universe, because it turns out

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.070
most of our cosmos is dark.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.390
Dark? Not as in night.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:40.680
Dark as in doesn't interact with light.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.560
And dark as in we don't really understand
it.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.380
The dark universe is made of two forces.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:54.420
Dark matter and dark energy.

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:01.150
In a way, the dark matter and dark energy
sort of oppose each other.

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:09.550
Dark matter has positive gravity that pulls
things together and dark energy has repulsive

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.290
gravity that pushes things apart.

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:15.830
Dark matter and dark energy are locked in
this epic struggle.

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:17.410
The dark matter is trying to bring things
together.

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:21.370
The dark energy is trying to drive
everything apart.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:27.190
All the objects in our daily experience, our
bodies, the air, the chair that you're

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:33.850
sitting in. The planets and stars, all of
that only adds up to 5% of the universe.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.850
It's the other 95% that is the dark stuff.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:41.150
The dark universe.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.430
To encapsulate that in one sentence.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.140
Dark matter is attractive.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:46.790
Dark energy is repulsive.

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:53.600
Since the dawn of time, two forces slugging
it out for control of the universe.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:05.800
It's a struggle that started 13.8 billion
years ago in the cosmic furnace of the Big

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:13.580
Bang. The infant universe was a super hot
ball of intense radiation.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:14.690
But suddenly.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.150
It started to transform.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:26.370
It cooled and expanded, triggering the birth
of the regular universe and the dark

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:33.570
universe. Scientists believe that both dark
matter and dark energy formed in the first

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.280
moments of the Big Bang.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:40.380
That would have formed probably just
fractions of a second after the Big Bang.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.510
Around the time that normal matter formed
and the processes that created the normal

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:50.790
matter we know all about similar processes
probably created the dark matter.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:57.960
During these first microseconds, the universe
was a hot, dense ball of matter and energy.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.230
There are two sides of the same coin.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:04.360
Matter can convert into energy.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:09.330
And energy can convert directly into matter.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:16.950
Visible particles of regular matter forming
atoms, planets, stars and us.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:19.760
And other particles.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:21.680
They are invisible.

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:23.540
They are dark matter.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:28.910
One of the big mysteries that we as
astronomers have to solve is what this dark

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:30.560
matter is. We just don't know.

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:37.250
The idea of a type of matter that you can't
see and that acts differently than normal

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:39.020
matter is sort of out there.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:40.250
It's sort of weird.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:46.640
It's like a crime scene, you know, that a
crime has been committed even though you

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.350
don't know the perpetrator yet.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.090
We see the hints.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:51.440
We see the signs.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:57.080
We see the signals that something funny is
happening in our universe, even though we

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:59.120
don't know exactly what's causing it.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.950
There are several contenders for what dark
matter could be.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:06.590
It could be normal matter that we just don't
see.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.420
Or it could be some sort of exotic matter, a
particle of some sort that we haven't

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:12.320
detected yet.

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:21.630
It could be a species of particle, like an
electron, like a proton, like a quark, but a

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:24.540
special kind that doesn't interact with
light.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:28.860
Dark energy has existed since the time of the
Big Bang.

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:30.690
Dark energy was always there.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:35.430
We don't know how much of it there was, but
it's possible that the same amount of dark

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:36.720
energy was always there.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:44.800
Dark energy is nothing more than a
placeholder name for this enormous gap in our

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:46.780
understanding of how the cosmos works.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:48.820
But we don't understand it at all.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:50.960
What's true.

00:05:53.480 --> 00:06:00.590
One potential answer to what dark energy
actually is may be found in so called empty

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:04.070
space. Could this be the source of dark
energy?

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:11.330
We used to think of space as just boring
emptiness, but now I think it's healthier to

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:13.580
think of space as a kind of substance.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:17.300
A substance that carries a strange type of
energy.

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:23.540
Every small region of space has a little bit
of energy in it just associated with the

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:24.800
vacuum itself.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:30.860
Vacuum energy is the idea that vacuum isn't
empty, that there is something there.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:33.740
There's an energy in it with a kind of
anti-gravity.

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:41.240
Perhaps this vacuum energy that pushes
against gravity.

00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:47.590
Is the mysterious dark energy we simply
don't know.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:53.430
We're not sure that the dark energy is the
vacuum energy.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:57.930
It could be a new type of energy that
permeates all of space.

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:00.060
This is what we're trying to measure now.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:06.510
Dark energy and dark matter forged in the
intense heat of the Big Bang.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:10.650
Opposing forces, one attractive, one
repulsive.

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:17.430
But together over 13.8 billion years, they
will shape the history.

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:18.390
Of the universe.

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:26.430
As the cosmic Web evolves into a more complex
structure.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:32.710
Gaps form between the filaments.

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:34.380
The cosmic voids.

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:43.330
The cosmic voids formed because other more
dense regions of the universe gravitationally

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:49.600
stole material away from them, so the dense
parts of the universe accumulated more matter

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:54.220
at the expense of the less dense parts which
then became voids.

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:56.740
Dark energy played no role.

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:58.150
It was insignificant.

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:05.290
But at some stage in the 14 billion years
since the Big Bang, these roles became

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:10.800
reversed. Dark energy came to be the more
powerful force.

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:13.750
The question was when.

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:17.320
The answer came at the end of the 20th
century.

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:23.200
The beginning of the universe shaping it and
actually creating the conditions for us to be

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:29.890
here. And it's taking over the universe, and
eventually it may well destroy the universe.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:37.050
Our universe may be dominated by the long
struggle between dark matter and dark energy.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:43.290
But all of this conflict has led to a
creative outcome, an outcome for which we

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:45.120
should all be grateful.

